1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,339
Your CTO asks that question on an ordinary
morning, and the room goes quiet, because

2
00:00:09,379 --> 00:00:10,094
nobody knows.

3
00:00:10,434 --> 00:00:14,958
There are two possible answers and both
are bad. If the engineer's account was

4
00:00:14,998 --> 00:00:19,756
removed from the organization, the job is
already failing and somebody will notice

5
00:00:19,796 --> 00:00:24,378
soon. If the account is still a member,
the job works, with a former employee's

6
00:00:24,418 --> 00:00:27,830
access, through a token that
appears in nobody's inventory.

7
00:00:27,870 --> 00:00:30,580
A token is a string that
stands for an account.

8
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:34,712
The right answer would have been:
"as an app that nobody can leave".

9
00:00:34,752 --> 00:00:39,334
This video is about making identities
deliberate: yours, when you have two of them

10
00:00:39,374 --> 00:00:44,011
on one laptop, and a machine's, when the
machine must outlive the people who set it

11
00:00:44,051 --> 00:00:47,562
up. Hold on to the nightly sync.
You'll redesign it at the end.

12
00:00:49,402 --> 00:00:53,502
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:53,542 --> 00:00:56,624
Four sections of Chapter
16 today, each short.

14
00:00:56,964 --> 00:01:02,466
First, two GitHub identities on one
machine, a personal and a work account,

15
00:01:02,506 --> 00:01:07,934
configured so that the directory a
repository lives in decides everything.

16
00:01:07,974 --> 00:01:14,068
This is the demonstration of the video:
labs/ch16/two-identities.sh, and the replay

17
00:01:14,108 --> 00:01:20,127
of the optional Lab 20.4. The replays use
a stand-in for ssh and reach no network.

18
00:01:20,467 --> 00:01:25,348
Second, credentials for machines: deploy
keys, GitHub Apps, OAuth apps, as the

19
00:01:25,388 --> 00:01:26,735
section compares them.

20
00:01:27,075 --> 00:01:31,835
Third, what SAML single sign-on and
mandatory two-factor authentication add.

21
00:01:32,175 --> 00:01:37,509
Fourth, the SSH changes of the fourteenth
of October 2026 and the thirteenth of

22
00:01:37,549 --> 00:01:43,087
January 2027. A note on that last part:
these dates come after the baseline of the

23
00:01:43,127 --> 00:01:48,665
textbook, which is the first of October
2026. I quote them from section 16.16, and

24
00:01:48,705 --> 00:01:54,379
the production notes require that they are
re-verified against GitHub's changelog on

25
00:01:54,419 --> 00:01:55,808
the day of recording.

26
00:01:56,148 --> 00:02:01,157
There's no GitHub walkthrough in this
video. The commands shown set global Git

27
00:02:01,197 --> 00:02:06,531
configuration inside the sandbox. On your
own machine, git config set --global is 🟡

28
00:02:06,571 --> 00:02:09,573
CAUTION because it applies
to every repository.

29
00:02:11,413 --> 00:02:12,826
After this video you can:

30
00:02:13,166 --> 00:02:17,627
Configure a personal and a work identity
so that each repository uses the right key

31
00:02:17,667 --> 00:02:19,200
and the right commit address.

32
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:22,526
Prove from a repository which
identity a push will use.

33
00:02:22,866 --> 00:02:27,149
Choose between a deploy key, a GitHub App
and an OAuth app for a machine, as the

34
00:02:27,189 --> 00:02:28,338
section compares them.

35
00:02:28,678 --> 00:02:33,398
Say what single sign-on authorization and
mandatory two-factor authentication add.

36
00:02:33,738 --> 00:02:38,267
State what changes for SSH users on
the two dates and who is affected.

37
00:02:40,107 --> 00:02:45,114
Two identities. In one sentence: give each
account its own key and its own ssh host

38
00:02:45,154 --> 00:02:50,100
alias, and let the directory a repository
lives in choose the alias and the commit

39
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:50,465
email.

40
00:02:50,805 --> 00:02:55,801
Why is this needed at all? Recall the three
independent identities from video 118:

41
00:02:55,841 --> 00:03:00,345
the commit identity, the authentication
identity and the signing identity.

42
00:03:00,385 --> 00:03:04,643
With two accounts on one laptop, each
of them can be wrong separately.

43
00:03:04,683 --> 00:03:09,003
A commit can go to the employer's
repository under your personal email.

44
00:03:09,043 --> 00:03:13,670
A push to your personal repository can
be authenticated as the work account.

45
00:03:14,010 --> 00:03:19,198
GitHub's guide for several accounts gives
two building blocks: Host aliases with

46
00:03:19,238 --> 00:03:23,707
IdentityFile and IdentitiesOnly,
which you saw in the last video, and

47
00:03:23,747 --> 00:03:27,432
url.<alias>.insteadOf, which
sends URLs through an alias.

48
00:03:27,472 --> 00:03:32,463
Combined with includeIf, the conditional
include you met in the configuration

49
00:03:32,503 --> 00:03:37,691
videos, one file holds everything that
makes a repository a work repository: the

50
00:03:37,731 --> 00:03:42,658
email, and the URL rewrite. Both kinds
of repository keep the canonical URL,

51
00:03:42,698 --> 00:03:47,951
git@github.com:OWNER/REPO.git. Inside the
work directory, the include applies and

52
00:03:47,991 --> 00:03:50,630
the effective URL goes through the alias.

53
00:03:50,970 --> 00:03:55,177
Over HTTPS the equivalent is
one credential per account:

54
00:03:55,217 --> 00:04:00,788
credential.https://github.com.username
inside the include, or useHttpPath.

55
00:04:00,828 --> 00:04:07,158
The GitHub CLI holds several accounts per
host and gh auth switch changes the active

56
00:04:07,198 --> 00:04:13,224
one. That switch is global, not per
directory, which is the reason to prefer SSH

57
00:04:13,264 --> 00:04:17,547
aliases when you work in both
identities on the same day.

58
00:04:17,887 --> 00:04:22,831
The failure mode of the design: a work
repository cloned outside the work directory

59
00:04:22,871 --> 00:04:27,455
escapes a rule keyed on the directory.
Lab 20.4 breaks the setup that way and

60
00:04:27,495 --> 00:04:30,638
repairs it with an includeIf
keyed on the remote URL.

61
00:04:30,978 --> 00:04:35,601
Now credentials for machines. A job that
runs without you needs an identity that

62
00:04:35,641 --> 00:04:36,184
isn't you.

63
00:04:36,524 --> 00:04:40,813
A deploy key: an SSH key attached
to one repository, not to a person.

64
00:04:40,853 --> 00:04:44,516
Read-only unless "Allow write
access" is chosen. No expiry.

65
00:04:44,556 --> 00:04:49,535
It can't be reused for a second repository,
usually has no passphrase, and keeps

66
00:04:49,575 --> 00:04:54,681
working after the person who added it
leaves. And from the chapter's edge cases: a

67
00:04:54,721 --> 00:04:59,513
deploy key with write access can do what
an admin collaborator can do in that

68
00:04:59,553 --> 00:05:04,721
repository, according to GitHub's page.
Treat it like a password to the repository.

69
00:05:05,061 --> 00:05:10,086
A GitHub App installation token: scoped to
the repositories and permissions of the

70
00:05:10,126 --> 00:05:13,916
installation, one hour, not tied
to a user, consuming no seat.

71
00:05:13,956 --> 00:05:18,857
It's GitHub's stated preference for
integrations. A GitHub App is an integration

72
00:05:18,897 --> 00:05:20,834
that acts with its own identity.

73
00:05:21,174 --> 00:05:24,756
A GitHub App user token: what
both the app and the user may do.

74
00:05:24,796 --> 00:05:27,516
Eight hours, with a refresh
token of six months.

75
00:05:27,856 --> 00:05:32,893
An OAuth app token: the scopes granted by
the user, across everything that user can

76
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:37,786
reach. An OAuth app that only needs to
read still asks for the broad repo scope.

77
00:05:38,126 --> 00:05:42,069
A machine user with a token or key:
a user account for automation.

78
00:05:42,109 --> 00:05:46,595
It occupies a seat, and somebody has
to own its password and second factor.

79
00:05:46,935 --> 00:05:50,211
And GITHUB_TOKEN: the workflow's
repository, for the job.

80
00:05:50,551 --> 00:05:55,321
The choice, as the section puts it: a
server that needs several repositories with

81
00:05:55,361 --> 00:06:00,309
deploy keys needs one alias per repository.
At that point an app is the better tool:

82
00:06:00,349 --> 00:06:05,000
one installation, short-lived tokens, and
a permission list that an auditor can

83
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:05,297
read.

84
00:06:05,637 --> 00:06:10,260
Quick quiz. A nightly job needs to
read one repository, and nothing else.

85
00:06:10,300 --> 00:06:15,625
Which credential is the smallest that does
it: a personal token, a deploy key, or an

86
00:06:15,665 --> 00:06:17,733
OAuth app token? Say it out loud.

87
00:06:22,573 --> 00:06:27,169
A deploy key: one repository, read-only
unless you allow writes, and tied to no

88
00:06:27,209 --> 00:06:31,629
person. If you said a personal token,
that's the nightly sync from the hook.

89
00:06:31,969 --> 00:06:37,512
Two-factor authentication. Since March 2023
GitHub requires everyone who contributes

90
00:06:37,552 --> 00:06:42,697
code on GitHub.com to enable it. Accounts
are enrolled in groups with a 45-day

91
00:06:42,737 --> 00:06:47,948
window. It protects the browser sign-in.
Tokens and SSH keys are used without a

92
00:06:47,988 --> 00:06:53,399
second factor, which is why they must be
guarded and, where possible, short-lived.

93
00:06:53,739 --> 00:06:57,042
SAML single sign-on is an
Enterprise Cloud feature.

94
00:06:57,082 --> 00:07:01,829
In an organization that uses it, a
credential must be authorized for that

95
00:07:01,869 --> 00:07:06,812
organization in addition to being valid.
A classic token is authorized after

96
00:07:06,852 --> 00:07:11,139
creation, per organization. A
fine-grained token, during creation.

97
00:07:11,179 --> 00:07:13,041
An SSH key, per organization.

98
00:07:13,381 --> 00:07:18,289
Deploy keys, installation tokens and
GITHUB_TOKEN need no authorization.

99
00:07:18,329 --> 00:07:23,786
An owner can revoke the authorization,
and a revoked SSH key can't be authorized

100
00:07:23,826 --> 00:07:29,559
again: a new key is needed. Through the REST
API, an unauthorized classic token gets

101
00:07:29,599 --> 00:07:35,194
404 or 403, and a 403 carries an X-GitHub-SSO
header with the URL to authorize it.

102
00:07:35,534 --> 00:07:40,589
One caveat the textbook marks unverified:
the text Git shows when an SSH key or

103
00:07:40,629 --> 00:07:45,878
token is not authorized for single sign-on
is not quoted in any documentation page

104
00:07:45,918 --> 00:07:50,457
the course found. The documented,
citable behaviour is the REST header.

105
00:07:50,797 --> 00:07:55,185
The SSH changes, announced on the
twenty-second of September 2026.

106
00:07:55,225 --> 00:08:00,820
Every date in this list lies after the course
baseline of the first of October 2026,

107
00:08:00,860 --> 00:08:05,046
so this is what GitHub announced,
not what the course observed.

108
00:08:05,086 --> 00:08:08,132
Check the changelog on
the day you watch this.

109
00:08:08,472 --> 00:08:14,071
The fourteenth of October 2026, as announced:
RSA keys uploaded after this date must

110
00:08:14,111 --> 00:08:19,509
be at least 3072 bits, for authentication
and for signing, and a post-quantum key

111
00:08:19,549 --> 00:08:23,872
exchange is to be enabled. Affected:
anyone adding a new RSA key.

112
00:08:24,212 --> 00:08:29,511
The fourth of November and the ninth of
December 2026, as announced: brownouts of

113
00:08:29,551 --> 00:08:34,388
the two removals that follow. Affected:
very old SSH clients, temporarily.

114
00:08:34,728 --> 00:08:40,463
The thirteenth of January 2027, as announced:
the ssh-rsa signature type, which is

115
00:08:40,503 --> 00:08:44,758
RSA with SHA-1, and one older
key exchange are to be removed.

116
00:08:44,798 --> 00:08:50,673
Affected: clients that can't sign RSA with
SHA-2, meaning OpenSSH older than 7.2 and

117
00:08:50,713 --> 00:08:52,292
old embedded libraries.

118
00:08:52,632 --> 00:08:58,955
Three points prevent wrong conclusions.
The signature type ssh-rsa is not the key

119
00:08:58,995 --> 00:09:05,239
type ssh-rsa: an existing RSA key keeps
working as long as the client signs with

120
00:09:05,279 --> 00:09:10,973
rsa-sha2-256 or rsa-sha2-512, which
current clients choose by themselves.

121
00:09:11,013 --> 00:09:17,336
Ed25519 and ECDSA keys aren't affected at
all. And HTTPS remotes aren't affected.

122
00:09:17,676 --> 00:09:23,449
What to check: ssh -V for the client version,
and ssh-keygen -l -f on a public key

123
00:09:23,489 --> 00:09:29,049
for its type and size. The machines to
worry about aren't laptops but old build

124
00:09:29,089 --> 00:09:32,309
agents, appliances and
libraries inside tools.

125
00:09:32,649 --> 00:09:37,311
And the caveat, unverified in the book:
the announcement does not state a minimum

126
00:09:37,351 --> 00:09:42,129
size for RSA keys that are already uploaded,
and GitHub publishes no single list of

127
00:09:42,169 --> 00:09:43,290
supported key types.

128
00:09:45,130 --> 00:09:49,811
Think of two wallets, one personal and
one from your employer, and a rule about

129
00:09:49,851 --> 00:09:54,055
which wallet you carry: the one that
belongs to the building you're in.

130
00:09:54,095 --> 00:09:58,895
Each wallet holds a key card, which is the
SSH key, and a business card, which is

131
00:09:58,935 --> 00:10:01,943
the commit email. You never
choose per transaction.

132
00:10:01,983 --> 00:10:03,198
The building decides.

133
00:10:03,538 --> 00:10:08,122
The model breaks exactly where the lab
breaks it: a work meeting held in a café.

134
00:10:08,162 --> 00:10:12,977
A repository cloned outside the work directory
is in no building, and the rule keyed

135
00:10:13,017 --> 00:10:17,543
on the directory picks the personal wallet.
That's why the lab ends with a rule

136
00:10:17,583 --> 00:10:21,762
keyed on the remote URL, on what the
repository is, not on where it sits.

137
00:10:22,102 --> 00:10:26,558
For machines the model is simpler: a
machine should carry a card issued to the

138
00:10:26,598 --> 00:10:27,019
machine.

139
00:10:28,859 --> 00:10:33,774
Read the personal row: plain github.com,
the personal key, the personal address, all

140
00:10:33,814 --> 00:10:38,199
from the global configuration and the
first block of the SSH configuration.

141
00:10:38,539 --> 00:10:43,741
Read the work row: the alias github-work,
which resolves to the same host with the

142
00:10:43,781 --> 00:10:48,792
work key. Then the work address. And the
last column says where both come from.

143
00:10:48,832 --> 00:10:52,373
One included file sets the
address and rewrites the URL.

144
00:10:52,413 --> 00:10:55,442
The SSH configuration maps
the alias to the key.

145
00:10:55,782 --> 00:11:00,538
The two lines at the bottom are the trick.
The stored URL is canonical in both.

146
00:11:00,578 --> 00:11:02,421
Only the effective URL differs.

147
00:11:06,761 --> 00:11:12,428
The global identity is the personal one.
The include applies to repositories under

148
00:11:12,468 --> 00:11:18,275
~/work/. The included file has two sections:
a different email, and the URL rewrite.

149
00:11:18,615 --> 00:11:23,957
The personal repository: the personal
address, and the stored and the effective URL

150
00:11:23,997 --> 00:11:28,431
are the same. The stand-in for ssh
shows that Git would start ssh for

151
00:11:28,471 --> 00:11:29,404
git@github.com.

152
00:11:29,744 --> 00:11:34,837
In the work repository, git config get
remote.origin.url and git remote get-url

153
00:11:34,877 --> 00:11:39,061
origin are two different questions.
Will they print the same URL?

154
00:11:39,101 --> 00:11:40,815
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

155
00:11:45,655 --> 00:11:51,007
They differ. The stored URL is unchanged.
The effective URL goes through the alias.

156
00:11:51,047 --> 00:11:56,009
And Git would start ssh for git@github-work.
--show-origin names the file the

157
00:11:56,049 --> 00:12:01,336
address came from: the included one. If
you expected the same URL twice, you're in

158
00:12:01,376 --> 00:12:06,273
good company: one command reads what is
stored, the other what Git will use.

159
00:12:06,613 --> 00:12:10,673
These four commands are the proof
the second objective asks for.

160
00:12:10,713 --> 00:12:15,990
From inside any repository: the address and
its origin, the effective URL, and what

161
00:12:16,030 --> 00:12:17,656
ssh would be asked to run.

162
00:12:17,996 --> 00:12:23,959
The SSH side, with ssh -G as in the last
video: the same host name for both, a

163
00:12:23,999 --> 00:12:28,347
different identity file for
each, and identitiesonly yes.

164
00:12:33,887 --> 00:12:37,441
One commit in each repository,
each with the right address.

165
00:12:37,481 --> 00:12:42,558
Three things changed together: the commit
email, the URL and, through the alias, the

166
00:12:42,598 --> 00:12:47,432
key. They are still three independent
identities. The include file is what keeps

167
00:12:47,472 --> 00:12:48,223
them aligned.

168
00:12:51,063 --> 00:12:55,657
The same proof in the lab's sandbox.
After this step the lab clones a work

169
00:12:55,697 --> 00:13:00,541
repository outside ~/work/ and asks you
what address and what key it will use.

170
00:13:00,581 --> 00:13:04,861
Predict it before you run it. The
failure and the recovery are yours.

171
00:13:05,201 --> 00:13:09,273
The first prints the client
version: compare it with 7.2.

172
00:13:09,313 --> 00:13:15,332
The second prints a key's size and type.
For an RSA key you plan to upload after the

173
00:13:15,372 --> 00:13:20,237
fourteenth of October 2026, the
announced minimum size is 3072 bits.

174
00:13:20,577 --> 00:13:26,168
Try it now, thirty seconds, in your
normal shell. Run ssh -V and compare your

175
00:13:26,208 --> 00:13:28,215
version with 7.2. I'll wait.

176
00:13:33,055 --> 00:13:37,937
If yours is 7.2 or newer, your laptop
isn't the machine to worry about.

177
00:13:37,977 --> 00:13:39,671
The old build agents are.

178
00:13:41,511 --> 00:13:43,159
Five mistakes to watch for.

179
00:13:43,499 --> 00:13:47,727
Pushing to the employer's repository
with the personal email in the commits.

180
00:13:47,767 --> 00:13:52,389
Root cause: the commit identity is independent
of authentication; nothing checks it

181
00:13:52,429 --> 00:13:54,523
unless you configure it per directory.

182
00:13:54,863 --> 00:13:59,932
Keying the work identity on the directory
and cloning a work repository elsewhere.

183
00:13:59,972 --> 00:14:04,978
Root cause: includeIf.gitdir matches the
location; a rule keyed on the remote URL

184
00:14:05,018 --> 00:14:06,411
matches the repository.

185
00:14:06,751 --> 00:14:11,672
Running a nightly job with a personal token.
Root cause: the job then authenticates

186
00:14:11,712 --> 00:14:16,693
as a person, and its access follows that
person's membership, not the job's purpose.

187
00:14:17,033 --> 00:14:21,001
Concluding that RSA keys stop
working on 13 January 2027.

188
00:14:21,041 --> 00:14:26,767
Root cause: the announcement removes the
signature type ssh-rsa, not the key type;

189
00:14:26,807 --> 00:14:29,017
current clients sign with SHA-2.

190
00:14:29,357 --> 00:14:33,508
Assuming a valid token works in an
organization with single sign-on.

191
00:14:33,548 --> 00:14:38,007
Root cause: the credential must also
be authorized for that organization.

192
00:14:39,847 --> 00:14:42,993
Now, out of the lab. The
nightly sync from the hook.

193
00:14:43,033 --> 00:14:47,649
The team finds the job's credential: a
classic personal token created by the

194
00:14:47,689 --> 00:14:52,550
engineer who left. The account is still
a member of the organization, so the job

195
00:14:52,590 --> 00:14:55,675
works, and the token appears
in nobody's inventory.

196
00:14:55,715 --> 00:15:00,147
So that's what the job authenticates
as today: a person who left in June.

197
00:15:00,487 --> 00:15:04,838
They replace it with a GitHub App
installed on the two repositories the sync

198
00:15:04,878 --> 00:15:09,517
touches, with the permissions the sync
needs. The job now obtains an installation

199
00:15:09,557 --> 00:15:14,197
token that lives one hour. Nobody's departure
changes it, and an auditor can read

200
00:15:14,237 --> 00:15:18,876
its permission list. For a job that needed
one repository read-only, a deploy key

201
00:15:18,916 --> 00:15:21,187
would have been the minimum improvement.

202
00:15:21,527 --> 00:15:26,510
For the calendar, the same team lists
machines, not people: every build agent,

203
00:15:26,550 --> 00:15:31,663
appliance and tool with an embedded SSH
library, with the output of ssh -V where

204
00:15:31,703 --> 00:15:34,110
there is one. Laptops aren't the risk.

205
00:15:35,950 --> 00:15:40,577
Your turn. Do Lab 20.4, "Two identities
on one machine (optional)", in

206
00:15:40,617 --> 00:15:45,645
lab-manual/m20-authentication-ssh.md.
Before each proof command, predict the

207
00:15:45,685 --> 00:15:50,178
address, the effective URL and the
host that ssh would be asked for.

208
00:15:50,218 --> 00:15:54,846
Before the failure scenario, predict
which of the three will be wrong.

209
00:15:55,186 --> 00:16:01,858
The challenge is Exercise 20.5, "The
push works, the address is wrong", in

